# Reflecting Mirror changes a spell's target only "if that target is you" - that spell's own target, as the ability
# resolves. Bot1 casts Stream of Life (X 1) at itself, then Lightning Bolt at Bot2 on top of it, and reflects its own
# Bolt, paying exactly twice its mana value (the two Moxen left, the pool empty after). The Mirror may target the Bolt (a
# spell with a single target), but the Bolt's target is Bot2, so nothing changes and Bot2 takes the 3 damage. Were the
# "if" ignored, or read off the Stream of Life under it, which does target Bot1, the Bolt would turn on Bot1, the only
# other player.

[Bot1 Deck]
Mox Pearl
Mox Jet
Mox Ruby
Mox Emerald
Reflecting Mirror
Forest
Stream of Life
Lightning Bolt
Island x10

[Bot2 Deck]
Island x20

[Actions]
Bot1.Hand.Forest.Play
Bot1.Hand.Mox Pearl.Cast
Bot1.Pass
Bot1.Hand.Mox Jet.Cast
Bot1.Pass
Bot1.Hand.Mox Ruby.Cast
Bot1.Pass
Bot1.Hand.Mox Emerald.Cast
Bot1.Pass
Bot1.Battlefield.Mox Pearl.Activate
Bot1.Battlefield.Mox Jet.Activate
Bot1.Battlefield.Mox Ruby.Activate
Bot1.Battlefield.Mox Emerald.Activate
Bot1.Hand.Reflecting Mirror.Cast
Bot1.Pass
[T3 Bot1.Main1]
Bot1.Battlefield.Mox Emerald.Activate
Bot1.Battlefield.Forest.Activate
Bot1.Hand.Stream of Life.Cast(Bot1; X: 1)
Bot1.Battlefield.Mox Ruby.Activate
Bot1.Hand.Lightning Bolt.Cast(Bot2)
Bot1.Battlefield.Mox Pearl.Activate
Bot1.Battlefield.Mox Jet.Activate
Bot1.Battlefield.Reflecting Mirror.Activate(Stack.Lightning Bolt)
Bot1.Pass
? Bot1.Mana =
[T3 Bot1.Main2]

[Expect]
Bot1.Life = 21
Bot2.Life = 17
Bot1.Battlefield.Reflecting Mirror.Tapped = true
Bot1.Graveyard = Stream of Life; Lightning Bolt
